---
/**
* The four things that have to be true for any of the above to be worth doing, and three people
* saying they were.
*
* Deliberately placed after the run rather than before it. A reader who has just watched the product
* work has a specific set of doubts by this point — who owns the account, what happens if we leave,
* who is standing between us and the AWS bill — and these are those doubts, in the order they occur.
*
* This is also where the page leaves the run: the rail is behind it and the layout opens back out to
* full width, which is the structural way of saying the story is over and the argument has started.
*
* The quotes are trimmed from the customers' own words on the current site. Trimming removes whole
* sentences; it never rewrites one.
*/
---

<section class="a-trust a-shell">
  <p class="a-eyebrow">What stays true</p>
  <h2 class="a-title a-title--movement">Four things that do not depend on us</h2>

  <div class="a-trust__grid">
    <div class="a-trust__item a-reveal">
      <p class="a-trust__term">The CLI is open source</p>
      <p class="a-trust__desc">
        The thing that reads your project, builds it and deploys it is on GitHub. The Console is the paid part.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="a-trust__item a-reveal">
      <p class="a-trust__term">Your AWS account</p>
      <p class="a-trust__desc">
        Every resource is created in an account you own, under your own IAM. Close your Stacktape account and it all
        keeps running.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="a-trust__item a-reveal">
      <p class="a-trust__term">Plain CloudFormation</p>
      <p class="a-trust__desc">
        The output is a template AWS understands on its own. No proprietary runtime, no control plane to depend on.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="a-trust__item a-reveal">
      <p class="a-trust__term">No cost markup</p>
      <p class="a-trust__desc">
        AWS bills you directly for what you use. Stacktape is never a party to that transaction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="a-voices a-shell">
  <p class="a-eyebrow">In production</p>
  <h2 class="a-title a-title--movement">Teams already running on it</h2>
  <p class="a-lede">And when they need us, the median first response from support is eight minutes.</p>

  <div class="a-voices__grid">
    <figure class="a-voice a-reveal">
      <blockquote class="a-voice__quote">
        With Stacktape, we fast-tracked our AWS deployment process. Our development and production environments were
        operational in just two days.
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="a-voice__by">
        <span class="a-voice__name">Eric Allam</span>
        <span class="a-voice__role">CTO &amp; Founder, Trigger.dev</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="a-voice a-reveal">
      <blockquote class="a-voice__quote">
        They abstract away so much of the complexity of AWS, and let us focus on our application logic, instead of
        infrastructure configuration.
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="a-voice__by">
        <span class="a-voice__name">Henry Garrett</span>
        <span class="a-voice__role">Founding Engineer, Receipts</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="a-voice a-reveal">
      <blockquote class="a-voice__quote">
        It’s allowed our small team to efficiently handle environments using GitOps, all while keeping a tight rein on
        costs.
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="a-voice__by">
        <span class="a-voice__name">Rhys Williams</span>
        <span class="a-voice__role">CTO &amp; Founder, Lastmyle</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>
